// SPEC § Compile-time constants: a module `comptime` is "a module-level named constant (subject to the
// module `export { }` surface)". This fixture is that sentence's proof, and it exists because the
// sentence was false in four separate ways at once — reported by the first external project on kama.
//
// Naming a `comptime` here was rejected outright ("no such top-level declaration"). Worse, and unseen:
// a `comptime` lowered to `static const` in its OWN unit's `.c`, so even a sibling file in this same
// module could not link against it — and `kama check`, which folds the program into one unit, reported
// every such reference as fine. The corpus could not catch it because no fixture ever put a module
// constant and a second file in the same build.
export { CAP, get };

comptime int32 CAP = 8;
fn int32 get() { return CAP; }
